      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path:</strong> F6 (35% Iranian strike on US assets in 72h) anchors a continuing strike-retaliation cycle; background risk of nuclear facility strikes or regional spillover (Kuwait, UAE) rises with each exchange round.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path:</strong> Neither side achieves decisive military outcome — US mine-clearance operations and Iran's mine-redployment cycle produce a sustained stalemate with periodic exchange bursts, as characterized by NPR's "intermittent war" framing through September 2.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path:</strong> F8 (18% new ceasefire by Oct 1) and F9 (22% Khamenei framework offer by Oct 27) together define the de-escalation corridor — both requiring political decisions not currently observable in state-media or mediator channels.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN (descriptive, ~200 words) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>IRNA and Tasnim characterized the September 1-3 exchange exclusively as "deterrence operations" (عملیات بازدارنده), the IRGC framing standard for all kinetic operations in the ongoing war. Kayhan ran an unsigned front-page editorial on September 3 calling for "decisive response" to US strikes — unsigned Kayhan editorials in this format carry Supreme Leader-aligned editorial authority and are not attributed to named staff. Fars News Agency credited IRGC air defense units with "successful interceptions" of US drones over southwestern Iran; CENTCOM published no confirmation of any Iranian intercept of US assets. Press TV's English-language broadcast on September 2 listed Al Udeid base as a named Iranian strike target without confirming impact data, and characterized Iran's ballistic missile barrage as "precision operations against American aggression." → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>IRIB (state television) ran extended footage of IRGC launch operations on September 3 — a standard war-broadcast format present throughout the July-September active exchange period. Tasnim's diplomatic desk produced no article referencing FM Araghchi or SNSC engagement in negotiations between September 1 and September 17; state media made zero references to ceasefire talks across the entire reporting window. The complete diplomatic silence in Iranian state media across 17 days corroborates the low probability on new talks. → supports <span class="cite">F8</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

